1
00:00:02,160 --> 00:00:06,880
Let's try now to modify our solution 
by taking down the no preemption case. 

2
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:12,960
The idea behind what we are going to see next can 
be summarized as follows: If a philosopher does  

3
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:18,000
not manage to get the chopstick on the right, 
then he/she releases the chopstick on the left. 

4
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:23,600
This is how the solution looks like. Let's first 
introduce some data structures and methods. 

5
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:29,440
We define the type chopstick_structure 
so that it contains a binary semaphore  

6
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:34,240
initialized to 1 and a boolean variable 
called available initialized to true  

7
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:38,800
(which means that at the very beginning of the 
execution each chopstick_structure is available). 

8
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:45,360
We then define the trylock method so that, 
when passed a chopstick_structure the first  

9
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:51,440
thing it does is to wait on its binary semaphore.
After the wait, if the chopstick is available,  

10
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:56,880
it sets the variable to false and 
stores true as return value, otherwise  

11
00:00:56,880 --> 00:01:01,760
it stores false as return value. Finally, 
it sends a signal on the chopstick's binary  

12
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:07,280
semaphore and returns the return value.
The lock method simply enters a loop in  

13
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:12,880
which it stays until trylock returns true (i.e., 
when it finds the chopstick to be available). 

14
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:19,120
The release waits on the binary semaphore. 
Then, it sets the available variable to true  

15
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:21,600
and signals the binary semaphore.

16
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:28,320
We can now look at the solution as a whole. 
In their entry section, the threads define  

17
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:35,040
a while loop. The loop goes on as long as a local 
variable called holding_both_chopsticks is false. 

18
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:41,920
Inside the loop, the thread tries to lock the 
first chopstick. Based on the method we have  

19
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:48,480
introduced, it means it keeps running trylock as 
longs as it does not find the available variable  

20
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:56,240
of the chopstick it is trying to lock set to true.
After that, it runs trylock on the second  

21
00:01:56,240 --> 00:02:02,960
chopstick. If try lock returns true, then the 
chopstick available variable was true, and  

22
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:09,600
the thread sets holding_both_chopsticks to true, 
exits the loop, and enters the critical section. 

23
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:13,360
Otherwise, the thread 
releases the other chopstick. 

24
00:02:14,480 --> 00:02:21,520
After the critical section, each 
thread/philosopher runs release on both chopsticks  

25
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:26,720
and sets holding_both_chopsticks to false.
Then, the thread/philosopher enters the  

26
00:02:26,720 --> 00:02:30,480
remainder section, in which he/she 
can think instead of eating.

27
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:38,320
If you look at this solution carefully, you 
can see a thread enters its critical section  

28
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:44,160
only if both chopsticks are available and, 
when a thread finds the first chopstick to  

29
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:48,880
be availalbe but not the second one, 
the first is set to available again,  

30
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:55,920
which means in the next iteration of loop the 
thread will try again to get both chopsticks.

31
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:04,000
This solution is valid when it comes to mutual 
exclusion, and it also avoids deadlocks. 

32
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:09,600
It has 2 downsides, still. One is that a process 
can starve, since one process X could be the one  

33
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:15,600
that is always managing to get both chopsticks.
The other downside is that, while preventing  

34
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:18,800
deadlocks, this algorithm 
does not prevent livelocks. 

35
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:25,520
We have not discussed livelocks in details, but I 
just want to draw your attention on the fact that  

36
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:32,000
if there are two processes and if both processes 
do not manage to get the second chopstick  

37
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:36,480
it might happen that in some execution 
order (imagine the two processes are  

38
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:42,640
concurrently being executed on the same CPU) they 
both release the other chopstick and try again,  

39
00:03:42,640 --> 00:03:49,840
and this try and retry can go on 
for an unbounded number of times.

